Task 1: Fast-Forward Merge
The first two merges our team did were fast-forward merges because we made only updates and changes on the new branches (neat_branch & 
messy_branch) and not on main before merging over. Had we made additional updates and changes on main as well, then we would have to perform 
three-way merges.



Task 2: Conflicts
One scenario our group tested out that would have a conflict between two branches, develop and mb3 (messy branch 3), is when the title on the 
index.html file is changed to different things and commited on both branches. Because these changes happened on the same line of code in the 
same file, a conflict arose when we tried to merge. Using the VS code interface I manually resolved this error by selecting which version of 
the file I wanted - the title “im develop” from the develop branch version - and committing this change.
 
A scenario we tested that would not resolve in a conflict is when the body text is updated on the develop branch while the title is updated on 
the conflict_branch2. Becasue these changed took place on different lines of code (even if they’re within the same index.html file), Git accepts 
both changes and is able to merge the versions without conflict. 

